1. Executive summary

The Bio‑Integrity Simulation System (BISS‑v1.0) is a conceptual computational architecture designed to simulate organism‑level physiological states under strict cryptographic protection and autonomous integrity control. It integrates multi‑layer data ingestion, endogenous encryption, physiological modeling, and sovereign suprakernel governance to maintain bio‑integrity in the presence of internal and external perturbations.

This white sheet describes the architecture, data flows, mathematical foundations, and integrity mechanisms of BISS‑v1.0 as a non‑clinical, non‑diagnostic conceptual model intended for design exploration and theoretical simulation only.[^1]

2. Problem definition and context

2.1 Motivation

Modern physiological modeling systems often treat security, encryption, and integrity as external concerns layered on top of simulation engines. In parallel, cryptographic systems rarely model the dynamic, non‑linear behavior of living organisms. BISS‑v1.0 explores a unified architecture where:

Physiological state evolution and cryptographic protection are co‑designed.

Integrity is enforced by an autonomous suprakernel rather than external monitoring.

Simulation outputs are exportable only through controlled, encrypted interfaces.

2.2 Scope and non‑medical nature

BISS‑v1.0 is not intended for clinical decision‑making, diagnosis, treatment, or regulatory submission. It is a conceptual, non‑medical simulation framework for:

Exploring secure physiological modeling architectures.

Testing integrity behaviors under synthetic scenarios (e.g., cosmic flare, endocrine overload).

Serving as a design reference for future, more formalized systems.

3. System overview

3.1 High‑level architecture

BISS‑v1.0 is organized into four primary layers, governed by a Sovereign Autonomous Suprakernel (v4.0):

Layer 1 — Data IngestionSecure intake of encrypted telemetry, anonymized patient‑like data, and heterogeneous medical‑style datasets via staging buffers.

Layer 2 — Endogenous Encryption Kernel (TSECE Integration)Dual cryptographic pipelines for data‑at‑rest and data‑in‑use, including AES‑GCM engines and homomorphic encryption kernels.

Layer 3 — Physiological Modeling EngineMulti‑compartment metabolic solver, pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic (PK/PD) mesh, and system dynamics integration.

Layer 4 — Secure State Storage & ExportEncrypted state matrices, secure volatile memory, access‑controlled outputs, and filters for potentially identifiable information.

The suprakernel wraps these layers with autodidact diagnostics, integrity checks, and epoch validation, enforcing a closed, sovereign simulation environment.[^2][^3]

4. Data ingestion architecture (Layer 1)

4.1 Input classes

Encrypted telemetry streams: Synthetic physiological signals (e.g., heart‑rate‑like, hormone‑like, stress‑index‑like) delivered via encrypted channels.

Anonymized organism datasets: Abstracted, non‑identifiable data representing baseline states, parameter distributions, and cohort‑like variability.

Heterogeneous medical‑style datasets: Conceptual imports mimicking lab values, imaging‑derived metrics, or time‑series measurements, all treated as non‑clinical.

4.2 Secure staging buffers

All incoming data passes through secure staging buffers that:

Verify encryption and integrity tags.

Normalize units and time bases.

Enforce schema constraints before data enters the modeling engine.

This layer ensures that only structurally valid, cryptographically verified data can influence downstream physiological states.

5. Endogenous encryption kernel (Layer 2)

5.1 Dual cryptographic pipelines

Data‑at‑rest pipeline:Uses AES‑GCM‑like engines to encrypt stored state matrices, configuration sets, and logs. Integrity tags are bound to suprakernel epoch identifiers.

Data‑in‑use pipeline:Uses homomorphic‑style encryption kernels to allow limited computation on encrypted physiological variables without exposing raw values.

5.2 TSECE‑style integration

An Endogenous Encryption Kernel conceptually couples:

Internal physiological variables (e.g., hormone levels, organ states).

Cryptographic keys and policies.

Suprakernel integrity rules.

This coupling ensures that any attempt to modify physiological state outside allowed transitions is detectable at the cryptographic level.

5.3 Integrity and compliance principles

The design aligns conceptually with emerging guidelines for computational models—emphasizing credibility, understandability, reproducibility, and extensibility (CURE)—by making encryption and integrity central to the model rather than peripheral.[^3][^4]

